APLer wrote:
> 
> Why OO?

Because it fits better (more naturally) than any procedural language.

> it's a game which lends itself fine to C alone. Granted there
> *are* lists and other(?) dynamic memory usages in it. [...]

I suppose you have no OO experience, otherwise I cannot understand why
you mention lists in this context. (The container classes in C++, for
example, with all the other STL elements reflect - despite some class
inheritances - more of a functional paradigm than an object oriented.)
The OO aspect pops in when modelling Nethack in terms of objects (see
Kent's hints in this thread).

> [...] Just because OO is become "in" is no reason to switch to it.

Yes, it's no reason to switch to a language because it's currectly in
vogue. But the OO concept is beyond vogue, and I suggest to read some
more about that OO topic. (It's not bad to even start with the Simula
documents from around 1967 about the intentions of the language design.)

> Trying to write database code
> in C would be nuts, whereas trying to calculate pi in Lisp would be too.
> They have uses and times when they are simply not the best way to do
> things. I see no real reason for using OO other than the lists involved.
> Certainly nothing to warrant a switch to C++.

Apparently nothing what _you_ can currently see warrants a switch.
But what you see or not is hardly a criterion for such a decision.

Someone mentioned that the language decision should (or could?) be
postponed; yes, if there's a design from scratch, and this should IMO
be an OO design, then quite any OO language might qualify (depending
on other requirements; GUI, portability, whatever), if the existing
code base shall be extended then C is the natual choice, and if a
code refactoring (with optional local OO modifications) is the choice
then C++ might be set as a best choice.

